  One thing worth pulling forward: knowing notifications is the destination should change how I design #1. A task model
  with an append-only change log from the start makes #3 and #4 straightforward; bolting change tracking onto a model
  that only stores current state is a rewrite. That's a cheap decision now and an expensive one later, so I'd bake it in
  even though we're building #1 first.

  Before I brainstorm tasks core, there's a contradiction in the premise I need you to resolve.

  [1mThe fork:[0m a static page with no server can't notify user B that user A changed something — there's nowhere for that
  message to live or travel. So "users" is either plural or it isn't, and the answer sets the size of the whole project.

  - [1mSingle-user[0m — "tasks you care about" means your own. Notifications become due-date reminders, overdue nudges, maybe
    the Web Notifications API. Entirely buildable client-side, no server, no auth. Small and real.
  - [1mMulti-user[0m — you need a backend, a database, auth, and a delivery channel. That's a genuine web application, not a
    page. Notifications as you originally described them only exist in this world.

  Neither is wrong, but they share almost no code. Picking single-user and later needing multi-user means rewriting the
  persistence and identity layers wholesale — this is the expensive-to-reverse one. Picking multi-user means the first
  useful thing you can see is days of work away rather than hours.

  My recommendation: single-user, unless you know real collaboration is the point. The notification ideas that make an
  app feel alive — due soon, overdue, recurring — don't need other people, and you get something working today.
